==English==
{{was wotd|2007|November|21}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌnɪkəl ən ˈdaɪm/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-nickel and dime.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-nickel and dime.ogg|a=AU}}

===Alternative forms===
* [[nickel-and-dime]]

===Etymology 1===
From the names of two [[US]] [[coins]] of small value.

====Adjective====
{{head|en|adjectives}}

# {{lb|en|US|idiomatic|colloquial}} [[small-time|Small time]]; operating on a small [[scale]]; involving small amounts of [[money]]; [[petty]] or [[cheap]].
#: {{nearsyn|en|two-bit#Adjective|ten-cent#Adjective}}
#: {{ux|en|I bought my new ride from some '''nickel and dime''' used-car salesman.}}
#: {{ux|en|Don't waste your time with that; their operations are '''nickel and dime'''.}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|pres_3sg2=nickels and dimes|pres_ptc2=nickeling and diming|pres_ptc3=nickelling and diming|past2=nickeled and dimed|past3=nickelled and dimed}}

# {{lb|en|US|idiomatic|colloquial}} To charge, or be charged, several unexpected small amounts of [[money]], often in the form of [[fee]]s, taxes, or related expenses to a venture, which when taken as a whole add up to a significant unexpected cost.
#: {{ux|en|I got '''nickel and dimed''' to death by the phone company's sneaky extra charges.}}
#: {{ux|en|It seems like a great offer, but they will just '''nickel and dime''' you until you've spent more than retail anyway.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|work=The New York Times|title=‘Spending Money to Get Nothing’: The Latest on Resort Fees|author=Elaine Glusac|date= Oct. 11, 2023|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/10/11/travel/resort-fees.html|passage=Even as resort fees are under fire, the '''nickel-and-diming''' of travelers that airlines have adopted is seeping into hotel operations. Things that used to be complimentary, such as early check-in or late check-out, now often carry associated fees.}}
# {{lb|en|US|idiomatic|colloquial|figuratively}} To [[wear down]] in small increments; to [[quibble]] or [[obsess]] [[endlessly]] with (someone) over [[trifle]]s.

====See also====
* {{l|en|fleece}}

===Etymology 2===
From prison or criminal slang.

====Noun====
{{head|en|noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|slang}} Fifteen years.

===Etymology 3===
After retail stores where “everything costs a nickel or a dime”

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|and|Canada}} A [[retail]] store selling [[inexpensive]] items, especially one in which all items have a [[price]] near a [[nickel]] (US five cents) or a [[dime]] (US ten cents)
#: {{syn|en|five and ten|five and dime}}

====See also====
* [[dollar store]]

